aper.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the function is not continuous at $x=10$, it can't be differentiable at that point.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-101964" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101964" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101964" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/ce2ec4b51b4c55975bf25234fdfbfa98663ae95ef7ee2bd5a774c4e4d4655292.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  $x=0$ and $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-101994" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101994" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=1$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101994" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=0$ and $x=1$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ due to the existence of a vertical tangent in the interval?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the function $y=h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$ \nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=h(x).$\n\nThe other graphs do not have a vertical tangent inside the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=g(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The functions $y=p(x)$ and $y=q(x)$ are not defined at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-47669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/1ef7e9ce0d1db1e60721d5f44b094d23091fd42b02b3b93832f4b17523241c87.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/8e9b1f8848e09eb936116d39dc72b9d15bded2426b2e4de23f88e257cbe9d46f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/bf943c2d6f9281c62f82712485bce8ab06b7312e8156baf0b961e858e0f28ea6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/cf9beb1c95dc2d69b430a47be217b98b81bec65c3370099397ab70672f8cbe54.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/d297db86c650af71f2d116f65470aa46445aceede8f81462e22e1becb284736a.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/d297db86c650af71f2d116f65470aa46445aceede8f81462e22e1becb284736a.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1$, is differentiable at $x=2$, and has a vertical asymptote at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It  is continuous at $x=2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=2$ it is also  continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical asymptote at $x=3$, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at this point, hence it can't be differentiable at $x=3$ either." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-47670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$, and is differentiable at $x=-1$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It  is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is continuous at $x=-1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-101995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-101995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ due to the existence of a cusp?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $y=q(x)$ has a cusp at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$ So $y=q(x)$ is the correct answer.\n\nThe other graphs do not have a cusp within the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=g(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ and is therefore differentiable at every point in the interval.\n\n- The function $y=p(x)$ is not defined at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-47685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/306baf2d3626ebe7f72db4dc87c1f0c0ad5fd2cfa24aaf211e34a25721227cda.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/476f5e8313b5ef82143b00424786c384c3159e1de9586f102655abe13ff1d7d4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/ba2e2109b1cbf4271cc034f55a51382eab026e8316da4618a5479a24eef0ba43.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/c3eee876a16931f73e275222931da77be3d7828753dba53f5db511d0cd0abd94.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/fb89648926f52a41de06838b4380c70bd3892feb951a8c56bcda0022c887d34f.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/476f5e8313b5ef82143b00424786c384c3159e1de9586f102655abe13ff1d7d4.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 1, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is **not** continuous at $x=0$ but it is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-73267" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73267" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73267" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47681" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+1, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 3, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(-1,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47681" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each statement individually. \n\n- Statement I is false.  At $x=1$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=3,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ ($f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. Since  $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47681" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-47681" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47681" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47681" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-102159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=-1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-102159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the function $h(x)$ is differentiable for all $x\\ne 1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$\n\nThe other functions are not differentiable at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-102159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-102159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-102159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}"] ["math" "g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})"] ["math" "h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}"] ["math" "p(x)=\\sqrt[5]{x+1}"] ["math" "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & x<-1 \\\\ x-1,\\, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-102159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=-2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the function $p(x)$ is differentiable for all real number $x.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $p(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nAll other functions are not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=-2$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-2$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$has a vertical tangent at $x=-2$, and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2$ and hence it is not differentiable there." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-47680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+2}"] ["math" "g(x)=2|x+2|"] ["math" "h(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x+2}"] ["math" "p(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}x,\\, & x<-2 \\\\ x-2,\\, & x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-47680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "p(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x<1 \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- Statement I and II are true. Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at  $x=1.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x+1)=1+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is  continuous at $x=1$ . However, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=1\\,$  if  $\\,x<1$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>1$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f9af49b14e4ed975b26542dcc08e6f8972a92224969edcc797dc30d7a9517cd"} {"question" "q-73270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "q-73270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/state.json" "file_sha256" "9944d3e008ecdd2117d2486bb933b9a04b0fcd99b361ac922750fc34d4ad75ec"} {"question" "e-131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Is the function $y=f(x),$ plotted below, differentiable at $x=-1?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/fafd2847f21de3f9f35e05dac61e8a5e406ede40ef7fba014ca5c01eb6d6cdd6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/example-131.json" "file_sha256" "6779e3f9dcd988ce0c71e93397e04543de324e2e9cc2a118f6b59ac246125042"} {"question" "e-131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=-1.$\n\nThe function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Since it is not continuous at $x=-1,$ it is also not differentiable there." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/example-131.json" "file_sha256" "6779e3f9dcd988ce0c71e93397e04543de324e2e9cc2a118f6b59ac246125042"} {"question" "e-133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted below. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/59499238b529d4d583885cf2c06bae11991c7d3fb5f84f347158e774e2b4ae00.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/example-133.json" "file_sha256" "9b25c54e25921bf7f155b762ee56b96e4b47e3e7939cd19879faad8bc428774a"} {"question" "e-133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a vertical tangent, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has a removable discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0, 1, 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/example-133.json" "file_sha256" "9b25c54e25921bf7f155b762ee56b96e4b47e3e7939cd19879faad8bc428774a"} {"question" "e-132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions are differentiable at the point $x=-1?$\n\n- $f(x) = x^5+1$\n\n- $g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/example-132.json" "file_sha256" "9ed0acf4e898c578c662f10b56664f92508f69db6e4a34cf0df98f4fe7a08f0a"} {"question" "e-132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial and is therefore differentiable and continuous for all $x.$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=-1,$ so it is not differentiable at $x=-1$ either.\n\nIn conclusion, only $f(x)$ is differentiable at the point $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/example-132.json" "file_sha256" "9ed0acf4e898c578c662f10b56664f92508f69db6e4a34cf0df98f4fe7a08f0a"}] "usage" {"q-47669" [] "e-132" [] "e-131" [] "e-133" []} "basis" 862 "retractions" [[17592186115796 :question/problem "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x ∈ (-1, 1)$ due to the existence of a vertical tangent in the interval?"] [17592186114838 :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x)$, plotted below, differentiable at $x=-1$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1691/Source/Images/b9be6cb7e47a4541bf7daca4184816a7.png)"] [17592186114838 :question/worked-solution "The function $y=f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=-1$.\n\nThe function has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1$. Since it is not continuous at $x=-1$, it is also not differentiable there."] [17592186114840 :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted below. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1691/Source/Images/e2fa4c174a5001cf56d29ee8a17509dd.png)"] [17592186114840 :question/worked-solution "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a vertical tangent, so it's not differentiable there.\n- At $x=1$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n- At $x=2$ the function has a removable discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0, 1, 2$."] [17592186114839 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are differentiable at the point $x=-1$?\n\n- $f(x) = x^5+1$\n- $g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$"] [17592186114839 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial and is therefore differentiable and continuous for all $x$.\n- The function $g(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=-1$, so it is not differentiable at $x=-1$ either.\n\nIn conclusion, only $f(x)$ is differentiable at the point $x=-1$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186114148 17592186114149 17592186114150 17592186114151 17592186114152] "immutable_fields" [[17592186114147 :answer-field/correct] [17592186114147 :answer-field/id] [17592186114147 :answer-field/key] [17592186114147 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
